# The minimum total statement coverage this repo accepts, as a percentage.
#
# scripts/coverage-floor.sh sums covered and total statements straight out of
# coverage.out and fails if the ratio has fallen under the single bare number
# below. CI runs that check after `make test-coverage`; `make coverage-check`
# runs it locally.
#
# This number will not match `go tool cover -func`'s total line, which reads
# about six points higher. That tool drops any block not inside a top-level
# func declaration, and cmd/ccvault declares its sixteen cobra commands as
# package-level vars, so ~600 statements of largely untested CLI body are
# invisible to it. The script explains the measurement in full; the short
# version is that this figure agrees with `go test`'s own per-package numbers
# for all 21 packages and `-func`'s does not.
#
# This is a floor, not a target. It exists to catch coverage being *lost* --
# a deleted test, a new package with no tests, an assertion quietly dropped --
# without demanding anyone chase a percentage. Nothing here asks you to raise
# it to land a change.
#
# To raise it: run `make test-coverage`, read the total, and change the number
# in a commit of its own. Keeping that diff separate is the point of the file
# existing rather than the number living in ci.yml -- the floor moving is a
# decision someone made, and it should be reviewable as one.
#
# Do not lower this to make a red build green. A drop means coverage was lost;
# the number is the symptom. Lower it only when tests were deliberately
# removed, and say in the commit message which ones and why.
#
# Format: comments (#) and blank lines are ignored. Exactly one other line,
# holding a bare decimal number. Two numbers is an error, not a guess.
72.3
